om, to make a show of resisting it. A bit ironic then, if understandable, that the Irish novelist Sally Rooney is chafing at her fame. If you read only glancingly about literary culture, you could be forgiven for thinking that she is less a writer than a piece of $20 avocado toast, an excuse for people to express bafflement at the very idea of millennials and what they might be after or up to. Generational profiles often have broad truths to offer, but Rooney’s youth (the author of “Conversations With Friends” and “Normal People” is just now 30) seems to have short-circuited many observers. The discussion around her can feel so extraliterary that it’s easy to lose sight of what she does well on the page, which is quite a bit.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/02/books/01BOOKROONEY1/01BOOKROONEY1-articleLarge.png', 'height': 871, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/02/books/01BOOKROONEY1/01BOOKROONEY1-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 871}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/02/books/01BOOKROONEY1/01BOOKROONEY1-jumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 706, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/02/books/01BOOKROONEY1/01BOOKROONEY1-superJumbo.png', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1411, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/02/books/01BOOKROONEY1/01BOOKROONEY1-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/09/02/books/01BOOKROONEY1/01BOOKROONEY1-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/02/books/01BOOKROONEY1/01BOOKROONEY1-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sally Rooney and Love in the Time of General Systems Collapse', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Writer’s Stand-In Chews on Big Ideas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Beautiful World, Where Are You (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rooney, Sally (Author)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-01T17:39:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/713a1dce-d06d-5129-96f5-05164cfd7700', 'word_count': 1389, 'uri': 'nyt://article/713a1dce-d06d-5129-96f5-05164cfd7700'}, {'abstract': 'A millennial slacker reckons with his past — and his family of warriors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/01/movies/shang-chi-and-the-legend-of-the-ten-rings-review.html', 'snippet': 'A millennial slacker reckons with his past — and his family of warriors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Home is where the heart is. Unless you’re Shang-Chi. Then home is where your mother’s mystical secret village — and its dragon guardian — is. That’s the case in Marvel’s unsteady “Shang-Chi and the Legend of the Ten Rings,” directed by Destin Daniel Cretton with an obliging eye toward kung fu cinema, but not muc