T10:33:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'NYTNow', 'section_name': 'Briefing', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Leonhardt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3e7f014-feea-53d4-8733-a690c0ea84d0', 'word_count': 1606, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3e7f014-feea-53d4-8733-a690c0ea84d0'}, {'abstract': 'An Indigenous worker in Brazil finds a surreal portal of sorts to the culture of his forebears.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/02/movies/memory-house-review-seeking-a-niche-between-present-and-past.html', 'snippet': 'An Indigenous worker in Brazil finds a surreal portal of sorts to the culture of his forebears.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While I don’t exactly agree that the worst of times generate the most compelling art, political turmoil can certainly inspire some of the angriest work. Case in point: the recent surge of class-conscious films by a number of Brazilian directors, such as Kleber Mendonça Filho (“Bacurau”), responding to the government’s austerity policies and its willful neglect of the country’s cultural heritage.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/03/arts/02memory-house/merlin_193814037_6f8f954b-d924-47f0-9aed-e2da9d90ad8a-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/03/arts/02memory-house/merlin_193814037_6f8f954b-d924-47f0-9aed-e2da9d90ad8a-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/03/arts/02memory-house/merlin_193814037_6f8f954b-d924-47f0-9aed-e2da9d90ad8a-jumb