63f4bb9-713e-54ca-9405-85b16af4e328', 'word_count': 1410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a63f4bb9-713e-54ca-9405-85b16af4e328'}, {'abstract': 'Hurricane Ida raised the question of how well energy companies are prepared for extreme weather.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/05/business/the-week-in-business-power-outage-hurricane.html', 'snippet': 'Hurricane Ida raised the question of how well energy companies are prepared for extreme weather.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hurricane Ida made landfall on Sunday, leaving more than a million residential and commercial customers in Louisiana without power. And the outages raise questions about how well energy companies have prepared for extreme weather, which scientists believe is becoming more common because of climate change. Some energy experts said that Entergy, the state’s largest utility company, could have done more to protect its equipment. Last year, the company began operating a natural gas plant that was meant to provide electricity to New Orleans during times of high demand or in emergencies. But it could not move the energy to customers because of damage to its network of towers, poles and wires. It has asked state regulators to raise customers’ rates to cover repair costs for Ida, three hurricanes in 2020 and a winter storm this year.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/05/business/05Withinterest1-illo/05Withinterest1-illo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/05/business/05Withinterest1-illo/05Withinterest1-illo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/05/business/05Withinterest1-illo/05Withinterest1-illo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 614, 'wid