': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'audio-positive-escape', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Saturday Night Live (TV Program)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-07T18:31:26+0000', 'document_type': 'audiocontainer', 'news_desk': 'Podcasts', 'section_name': '', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://audiocontainer/b50d96e6-ce91-5f3e-bb37-3b2e005d251c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audiocontainer/b50d96e6-ce91-5f3e-bb37-3b2e005d251c'}, {'abstract': 'Even at her peak, she was hugely famous but never especially cool. But maybe we’re finally ready to heed her whispered call to awaken.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/07/magazine/enya-recommendation.html', 'snippet': 'Even at her peak, she was hugely famous but never especially cool. But maybe we’re finally ready to heed her whispered call to awaken.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the long drives through Ireland that peppered my childhood like bouts of flu, my father played songs from a small pool of classic albums. Many of these would be familiar to any Irishman from that time. The cheerful ribaldry of the Dubliners, Christy Moore’s “Live at the Point” and the earnest, heart-tugging confessionals of Eleanor McEvoy and Mary Black all soundtracked our winding trips through the unending swatches of green that formed the Irish countryside. But none of those artists struck me like my father’s personal favorite, Enya.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/12/magazine/12mag-LOR-1/12mag-LOR-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 437, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {