arassment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Content Type: Service', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-08T09:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Parenting', 'section_name': 'Parenting', 'byline': {'original': 'By Misha Valencia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Misha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Valencia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/846c38fa-471b-5304-a0cd-9cc768445628', 'word_count': 1295, 'uri': 'nyt://article/846c38fa-471b-5304-a0cd-9cc768445628'}, {'abstract': 'An air puffer can help stop biting. Use thick leather gloves anyway.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/08/magazine/catch-bats.html', 'snippet': 'An air puffer can help stop biting. Use thick leather gloves anyway.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Treat bats the way you would unvaccinated children,” says Winifred Frick, chief scientist at Bat Conservation International. Before Covid, biologists often blew on bats’ faces to get them to stop biting and on their underbellies to confirm their reproductive status. Don’t do that; you could inadvertently give a bat coronavirus. New bat-handling protocols suggest using an air-puffing tool like one of those baby-booger nasal aspirators to puff air on a bat instead. Also, wear a mask, get vaccinated and test negative for coronavirus before going out in the field.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/12/magazine/12mag-tip/12mag-tip-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/12/magazine/12mag-tip/12mag-tip-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/12/magazine/12mag-tip/12mag-tip-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/12/magazine/12mag-tip/12mag-tip-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/12/magazine/12mag-tip/12mag-tip-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/09/12/magazine/12mag-tip/12mag-tip-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/12/magazine/12mag-tip/12mag-tip-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Catch a Bat', 'kicker': 'Tip', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Catch a Bat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus Risks and Safety Concerns', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bats', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Content Type: Service', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-08T09:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malia Wollan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wollan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/008e5a76-62b7-51a1-8009-2cc4dac2e380', 'word_count': 396, 'uri': 'nyt://article/008e5a76-62b7-51a1-8009-2cc4dac2e380'}, {'abstract': 'Two veterans of Operation Enduring Freedom reflect on where the Sept. 11 attacks led the nation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/08/opinion/afghanistan-veterans-september-11.html', 'snippet': 'Two veterans of Operation Enduring Freedom reflect on where the Sept. 11 attacks led the nation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the world reflects on the anniversary of Sept. 11, what does the day of the attacks — and the 20 years of war it precipitated — feel like to America’s veterans? With the Afghanistan withdrawal suddenly reclaiming attention for the “forever” wars, is the 9/11 era finally over, on the home front and in America’s foreign policy? Jane Coaston brings together Kenneth Harbaugh and Michael Washington, two friends and veterans of Operation Enduring Freedom, to discuss the pax Americana, the 9/11 roots of today’s divide in the veteran community and the political weaponization of service members’ patriotism. Harbaugh is a former Navy pilot turned veterans’ advocate and podcaster. Washington is a former Marine and firefighter who today works as a licensed therapist for veterans and emergency workers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/08/opinion/08argument-911vets-image/08argument-911vets-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/08/opinion/08argument-911vets-image/08argument-911vets-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/08/opinion/08argument-911vets-image/08argument-911vets-image-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 900, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/08/opinion/08argument-911vets-image/08argument-911vets-image-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 900, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/08/opinion/08argument-911vets-image/08argument-911vets-image-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/09/08/opinion/08argument-911vets-image/08argument-911vets-image-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/08/opinion/08argument-911vets-image/08argument-911vets-image-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘You Don’t Bring Democracy at the Point of a Gun’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'audio-negative-tragedy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'audio-neutral-informative', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Suicide Prevention Lifeline', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-08T09:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': "By ‘The Argument'", 'person': [], 'organization': "The Argument'"}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/610d95e1-54ed-549d-8b25-16a0b2e29a2e', 'word_count': 373, 'uri': 'nyt://article/610d95e1-54ed-549d-8b25-16a0b2e29a2e'}, {'abstract': 'Tolerance and intolerance are taking on many different guises.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/08/opinion/us-elites-populism.html', 'snippet': 'Tolerance and intolerance are taking on many different guises.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A highly charged ideological transition reflecting a “massive four-decade-long shift in political values and attitudes among more educated people — a shift from concern with traditional materialist issues like redistribution to a concern for public goods like the environment and diversity” is a driving force in the battle between left and right, according to Richard Florida, an urbanologist at the University of Toronto.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/08/opinion/08edsall1/08edsall1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/08/opinion/08edsall1/08edsall1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/08/opinion/08edsall1/08edsall1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/08/opinion/08edsall1/08edsall1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/08/opinion/08edsall1/08edsall1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/09/08/opinion/08edsall1/08edsall1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/08/opinion/08edsall1/08edsall1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'One Thing We Can Agree On Is That We’re Becoming a Different Country', 'kicker': 'Guest Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Liberalism (US Politics)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2020', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minorities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whites', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservatism (US Politics)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACADEMIC FREEDOM', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Florida, Richard', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Haidt, Jonathan', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaufmann, Eric (1970- )', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodden, Jonathan', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sunstein, Cass R', 'rank': 18, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 19, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-08T09:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas B. Edsall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/77c7dbf3-81f1-5732-b279-c60a65621ea4', 'word_count': 2439, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77c7dbf3-81f1-5732-b279-c60a65621ea4'}, {'abstract': 'The great thing about egg rice is that it’s hardly cooking. If you can fry an egg, then you can make egg rice.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/08/magazine/egg-rice-recipe.html', 'snippet': 'The great thing about egg rice is that it’s hardly cooking. If you can fry an egg, then you can make egg rice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every two weeks, Christina Dang buys a carton of 18 eggs from the Costco near her home in San Diego. Most of these eggs go into a snack she makes for herself when she comes home from work: egg rice. She fries a single egg sunny-side up and places it on a small plate of leftover jasmine rice. Then, over the yolk, she pours a little Maggi sauce, a dark, savory potion of wheat protein. For Dang, who is a pharmacist, there’s a ritual to eating this simple but potent dish, one she has upheld since she was young. She breaks the yolk and mixes the Maggi into it, creating a makeshift gravy that drapes the rice in eggy gold. She pierces her fork straight down into the food, so she has an even distribution of Maggi-tinged yolk, crisp-edged egg white and steamed white rice, fluffy as a fresh-raked pile of leaves, on her tines. “I eat this at least once a day,” she told me.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/12/magazine/12mag-Eat/12mag-Eat-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/12/magazine/12mag-Eat/12mag-Eat-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 451}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/12/magazine/12mag-Eat/12mag-Eat-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 771, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/12/magazine/12mag-Eat/12mag-Eat-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1541, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/12/magazine/12mag-Eat/12mag-Eat-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/09/12/magazine/12mag-Eat/12mag-Eat-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/12/magazine/12mag-Eat/12mag-Eat-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Simple Perfection of Fried Eggs and White Rice', 'kicker': 'Eat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Simple Perfection of Fried Eggs and White Rice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Eggs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-08T09:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Kim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee724f43-8d5e-58ee-8a56-eed27a45909c', 'word_count': 933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee724f43-8d5e-58ee-8a56-eed27a45909c'}, {'abstract': 'Six experts on the consequences of missed learning — and what it means for this year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/08/magazine/what-will-school-really-look-like-this-fall.html', 'snippet': 'Six experts on the consequences of missed learning — and what it means for this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A year and a half into the pandemic, the crucial and irreplaceable role that school plays in students’ lives has never been clearer. In contrast to last fall, when school buildings in some parts of the country closed for long periods (mostly in blue cities and towns), a consensus has emerged this year in favor of bringing as many students as possible back to the classroom. But the country remains divided about which measures are required to do this safely. Infections are already forcing mass quarantines, and fear and high prevalence rates may further threaten in-person schooling once again — despite the indisputable evidence of the severe cost to kids. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '56', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/12/magazine/12mag-rountablehomepage/12mag-rountablehomepage-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLar