8T09:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sabrina Tavernise', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sabrina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tavernise', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4107f7fe-81ec-541a-934a-d7464904e2af', 'word_count': 1357, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4107f7fe-81ec-541a-934a-d7464904e2af'}, {'abstract': 'A community in Tijuana is a model of a physically and psychologically safe place for vulnerable migrants.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/08/opinion/mexico-border-LGBTQ-migrant-shelter.html', 'snippet': 'A community in Tijuana is a model of a physically and psychologically safe place for vulnerable migrants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TIJUANA, Mexico — Steam curls off a plate overloaded with fresh tamales as refugees gather around a buffet-style dinner table, laughing together on a chilly evening here. Nearby, a pregnant woman shoos away toddlers underfoot, and one woman flips the hair of a blond wig so grand, it practically sweeps the cracked concrete floor. Across the room, a jovial young man teases a group of excited boys who are lining up to hit a rainbow-shaped pinata — a nod to the L.G.T.B.Q.-friendly space of Casa de Luz, where everyone gathered for this posada celebration in December.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/30/opinion/00Fixes-Pixley-slide-0FRZ/00Fixes-Pixley-slide-0FRZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/30/opinion/00Fixes-Pixley-slide-0FRZ/00Fixes-Pixley-slide-0FRZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': '