'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d904a843-959e-578b-ba2f-70c7af7fa10c', 'word_count': 930, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d904a843-959e-578b-ba2f-70c7af7fa10c'}, {'abstract': 'After the attacks, American culture became one of prohibitions. Then the Iraq War made it difficult to address Sept. 11 on its own terms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/09/arts/why-art-struggled-to-address-the-horrors-of-9-11.html', 'snippet': 'After the attacks, American culture became one of prohibitions. Then the Iraq War made it difficult to address Sept. 11 on its own terms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Weeks after the towers fell, Jennifer Bartlett started painting. She had watched them collapse from her roof that September, and in her studio in the West Village she began depicting what almost no one wanted to depict, in her style of solid dots daubed into a grid of little squares. Toward the edges the dots are that distinctive cloudless blue, but most squares she overlaid with two dots, or three, the gray of the smoke superimposed on the red or saffron of the fireball. The dots became embers of exploded airplanes, or TV screen pixels (we had no smartphones then); they were papers raining down on the financial district and the Battery. Across two squares Bartlett placed a figure, stylized like in a cave painting, feet over head. A diver.', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/27/arts/00911culture-1/00911culture-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 427, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/08/27/arts/00911culture-1/00911culture-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 427}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/27/arts/00911culture-1/00911culture-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 729, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/27/arts/00911culture-1/00911culture-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1459, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/27/arts/00911culture-1/00911culture-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/08/27/arts/00911culture-1/00911culture-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/08/27/arts/00911culture-1/00911culture-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Art Struggled to Address the Horrors of 9/11', 'kicker': 'Critic’s notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Culture Shock', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-09T09:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Farago', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Farago', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/59223f69-fccb-5354-ba35-5bb6919a44f7', 'word_count': 1115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/59223f69-fccb-5354-ba35-5bb6919a44f7'}, {'abstract': 'Doubles partners Coco Gauff and Caty McNally credit fellow teenagers Leylah Fernandez and Emma Radacanu for motivating them as they’ve advanced to the semifinals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/09/sports/tennis/us-open-women.html', 'snippet': 'Doubles partners Coco Gauff and Caty McNally credit fellow teenagers Leylah Fernandez and Emma Radacanu for motivating them as they’ve advanced to the semifinals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Emma Raducanu and Leylah Fernandez have put themselves one match away from an improbable all-teenage U.S. Open women’s singles final, an even younger duo is just as close to taking the trophy in tandem.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/10/sports/10usopen-gauff-mcnally/merlin_194393943_05135b4b-6076-4007-9643-845b2dc1c5d1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/10/sports/10usopen-gauff-mcnally/merlin_194393943_05135b4b-6076-4007-9643-845b2dc1c5d1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/10/sports/10usopen-gauff-mcnally/merlin_194393943_05135b4b-6076-4007-9643-845b2dc1c5d1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/10/sports/10usopen-gauff-mcnally/merlin_194393943_05135b4b-6076-4007-9643-845b2dc1c5d1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/10/sports/10usopen-gauff-mcnally/merlin_194393943_05135b4b-6076-4007-9643-845b2d