: 'Astor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/435d4f01-8dc7-5370-bb70-9e333c7e7984', 'word_count': 341, 'uri': 'nyt://article/435d4f01-8dc7-5370-bb70-9e333c7e7984'}, {'abstract': 'While other directors edited out the twin towers from movies at the time, Spike Lee worked the tragedy into a story originally about other things.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/10/movies/25th-hour-spike-lee.html', 'snippet': 'While other directors edited out the twin towers from movies at the time, Spike Lee worked the tragedy into a story originally about other things.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Spike Lee came under fire last month for including 9/11 conspiracy theorists in his HBO documentary series “NYC Epicenters 9/11-2021½,” historians and others expressed disappointment that Lee had seemed to give credibility to long-debunked claims. (He subsequently edited them out.) But for those of us who’ve followed Lee’s career, and its intersection with that seminal New York event of 20 years ago, the initial decision was especially baffling — as Lee also directed what many consider the quintessential film about post-9/11 New York City.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/11/arts/10twentyfith-hour/10twentyfith-hour-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 250, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/11/arts/10twentyfith-hour/10twentyfith-hour-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 250}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/11/arts/10twentyfith-hour/10twentyfith-hour-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 427, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'ran