ion': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec6ac7c9-8703-5715-a8b9-77eef371c907', 'word_count': 1465, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec6ac7c9-8703-5715-a8b9-77eef371c907'}, {'abstract': 'A hotel heist is at the center of “Harlem Shuffle,” but the deeper sin is America’s handling of race and the decline of a thriving midcentury community.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/10/books/colson-whitehead-harlem-shuffle.html', 'snippet': 'A hotel heist is at the center of “Harlem Shuffle,” but the deeper sin is America’s handling of race and the decline of a thriving midcentury community.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Sometimes he slipped and his mind went thataway,” Colson Whitehead writes about Ray Carney, the crime-adjacent Harlem furniture salesman at the center of his new novel, “Harlem Shuffle.” Whitehead’s own mind has famously gone thataway through nine other books that don’t much resemble one another, but this time he’s hit upon a setup that will stick. He has said he may keep Ray going into another book, and it won’t take you long to figure out why.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/books/14BOOKWHITEHEAD1/14BOOKWHITEHEAD1-articleLarge.png', 'height': 898, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/15/books/14BOOKWHITEHEAD1/14BOOKWHITEHEAD1-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 898}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/books/14BOOKWHITEHEAD1/14BOOKWHITEHEAD1-jumbo.png', 'height': 1024, 'width': 685, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/books/14BOOKWHITEHEAD1/14BOOKWHITEHEAD1-superJumbo.png', 'height': 2048, 'width