e}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae2e770a-4b74-56ea-8af1-504d3938b71a', 'word_count': 1051, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae2e770a-4b74-56ea-8af1-504d3938b71a'}, {'abstract': 'Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon are sturdy features in the landscape.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/11/crosswords/variety-acrostic.html', 'snippet': 'Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon are sturdy features in the landscape.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ACROSTIC — Once again, Team Cox-Rathvon presents a passage that speaks to me personally: I have a scar on my right leg from one of the evil, evil plants that we read about today, and the author of the passage doesn’t sound too fond of them, either. That author is Mary Hunter Austin, a naturalist who wandered alone through the Southwest in the 19th century, encountering all manner of desert fauna and flora (in heavy skirts, and hopefully unscathed). The passage comes from a book called “The Land of Little Rain,” published in 1903; Austin meets with adversity but also expresses admiration for yuccas, coyotes, cactuses and everything that scratches out a life in such harsh environs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Variety: Acrostic', 'kicker': 'SUNDAY VARIETY COLUMN', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southwestern States (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-11T16:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Games', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caitlin Lovinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caitlin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lovinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60dd3a76-0bde-5f4a-8d91-78e2495761fd', 'word_count': 182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60dd3a76-0bde-5f4a-8d91-78e2495761fd'}, {'abstract':