son misstates which Coldplay song her children were brought out to hear Chris Martin perform. It was “Yellow,” not “Green Eyes.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: Sept. 12, 2021', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2021-09-12T04:35:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Corrections', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37ba557a-a28d-5ee7-b82f-67f87ab37708', 'word_count': 381, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37ba557a-a28d-5ee7-b82f-67f87ab37708'}, {'abstract': 'Of course it was a circus — the kind that makes sense in boxing these days.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/12/sports/boxing-trump-triller-belfort-holyfield.html', 'snippet': 'Of course it was a circus — the kind that makes sense in boxing these days.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a single zany sentence, this is how the once-promising summer of boxing ended: Triller, a social video app that is a much less popular version of TikTok, put on a pay-per-view fight between a 58-year-old Evander Holyfield (who hasn’t fought in a decade) and a 44-year-old mixed martial artist, Vitor Belfort — and paid former President Donald J. Trump and Donald Trump Jr. to serve as live commentators, all on the 20th anniversary of the Sept. 11 attacks.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/12/sports/12boxing-triller/merlin_194581398_e5be34df-feae-4efc-b23a-d96a07f36bb4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/12/sports/12boxing-triller/merlin_194581398_e5be34df-feae-4ef