alue': 'Supreme Court (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Jackson Women's Health Organization", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fitch, Lynn (1961- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-13T20:21:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Washington', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30e46a9a-46eb-5840-b52a-21a9943b5a2e', 'word_count': 900, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30e46a9a-46eb-5840-b52a-21a9943b5a2e'}, {'abstract': 'Helene Henderson’s latest book, “Malibu Farm: Sunrise to Sunset,” features a host of breezy recipes that conjure sunshine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/13/dining/cookbook-Malibu-Farm.html', 'snippet': 'Helene Henderson’s latest book, “Malibu Farm: Sunrise to Sunset,” features a host of breezy recipes that conjure sunshine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is beach season over? Not if you delve into “Malibu Farm Sunrise to Sunset,” the enthusiastic, lavishly photographed new cookbook from Helene Henderson, an owner of the waterfront Malibu Farm restaurants in California, Hawaii and elsewhere, including in New York on Pier 17 in the seaport district. The book, which tells the story of the restaurants, offers easy fare, often vegetable-forward and suitable for outdoor dining, like delicata squash baked with red lentils, a simple flat omelet with a salad plopped on top, and a salad that beckons fall with kale, apples and brussels sprouts. Her grilled mahi mahi with black olive vinaigrette is not the only fish recipe that calls for a mayonnaise or aioli coating to prevent sticking. Ms. Henderson is a native of Sweden, so she is not shy about using creamy sauces like the mustard one for sheet pan chicken breasts, and even in vegetarian recipes like mashed cauliflower with a browned cheese topping. There’s also a chapter, mostly with recipes for sweets, about fika, the Swedish coffee equivalent of teatime.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-book/13Burner-book-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 723, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-book/13Burner-book-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 723}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-book/13Burner-book-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 850, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-book/13Burner-book-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1700, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-book/13Burner-book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-book/13Burner-book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-book/13Burner-book-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beachy Fare You Can Cook Year-Round', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Cook: A Handy Source For Beach Fare', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-13T20:28:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56e9bcfc-a8cb-503c-acee-87162bb6360a', 'word_count': 215, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56e9bcfc-a8cb-503c-acee-87162bb6360a'}, {'abstract': 'The four “elevated” cocktails at Sona in the Flatiron district make flavor changes for cooler autumn weather.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/13/dining/gin-and-tonic-sona-restaurant.html', 'snippet': 'The four “elevated” cocktails at Sona in the Flatiron district make flavor changes for cooler autumn weather.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To some, the recipe for a gin and tonic is simple and sacrosanct: gin, high-quality tonic water and lime. But keep an open mind. Johnny Swet, the mixology consultant at the Indian restaurant Sona in the Flatiron district, has created four “elevated” gin and tonics that show the beauty in variation. The No. 3, for example, is made with Jin Jili India Darjeeling Dry Gin, grapefruit, elderflower, mint and star anise; the grapefruit makes it delicious. The fragrant No. 1 combines Hendricks gin with makrut lime leaves, coriander seeds and lime juice, though come fall, it will be varied with apple flavors. For the No. 4, which has cucumber, cumin, fennel and Plymouth Gin, the cucumber will soon be replaced with pear. The popular No. 2 will lose its muddled strawberries for persimmon, but the black peppercorns will still be in the generous balloon glass. Mr. Swet said he wanted to preserve the Indian tradition of gin and tonic, while adding spices and other ingredients used in Indian cooking, and also to make the drinks seasonal, not just for summer. He consulted with the restaurant’s chefs.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-Drink/merlin_194475441_cadc602b-29d9-4fbb-8867-98d17ed15150-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-Drink/merlin_194475441_cadc602b-29d9-4fbb-8867-98d17ed15150-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-Drink/merlin_194475441_cadc602b-29d9-4fbb-8867-98d17ed15150-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-Drink/merlin_194475441_cadc602b-29d9-4fbb-8867-98d17ed15150-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-Drink/merlin_194475441_cadc602b-29d9-4fbb-8867-98d17ed15150-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-Drink/merlin_194475441_cadc602b-29d9-4fbb-8867-98d17ed15150-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-Drink/merlin_194475441_cadc602b-29d9-4fbb-8867-98d17ed15150-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gin and Tonics That Change With the Seasons', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Explore: These Gin and Tonics Change With the Seasons', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gin (Liquor)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Indian Food (Cuisine)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cocktails and Mixed Drinks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sona (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-13T20:28:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28926365-8003-5ba8-9b8a-ebed2b16a5e0', 'word_count': 223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28926365-8003-5ba8-9b8a-ebed2b16a5e0'}, {'abstract': 'The writer Larissa Zimberoff will lead a virtual talk on how labs are growing new plant-based foods and changing what we eat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/13/dining/technically-food-talk-mofad.html', 'snippet': 'The writer Larissa Zimberoff will lead a virtual talk on how labs are growing new plant-based foods and changing what we eat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Science has been hard at work creating plant-based and cell-cultured proteins to bring to the table. To explore this, the Museum of Food and Drink in New York has assembled a panel led by Larissa Zimberoff, the author of “Technically Food: Inside Silicon Valley’s Mission to Change What We Eat,” to discuss the topic on Zoom. On the panel will be Michael Leonard, who has worked in research and development with mass-market brands; Patricia Bubner, whose expertise is in cell-cultured meats; and Eben Bayer, a biologist working on new, sustainable products.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-Talk/13Burner-Talk-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 902, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-Talk/13Burner-Talk-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 902}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-Talk/13Burner-Talk-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-Talk/13Burner-Talk-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1363, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-Talk/13Burner-Talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-Talk/13Burner-Talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/dining/13Burner-Talk/13Burner-Talk-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Discussing the Future of Food', 'kicker': 'Front Burner', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Learn: Panel to Discuss The Future of Food', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Food and Drink (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-13T20:28:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea2ee96e-5558-5c51-95fa-70e06a56149d', 'word_count': 142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea2ee96e-5558-5c51-95fa-70e06a56149d'}, {'abstract': 'He brought jazz (and later folk music) to Rhode Island, and made festivals as important as nightclubs and concert halls on jazz musicians’ itineraries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/13/arts/music/george-wein-dead.html', 'snippet': 'He brought jazz (and later folk music) to Rhode Island, and made festivals as important as nightclubs and concert halls on jazz musicians’ itineraries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'George Wein, the impresario who almost single-handedly turned the jazz festival into a worldwide phenomenon, died on Monday at his apartment in Manhattan. He was 95. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/14/obituaries/13Wein2-print2/merlin_164402055_e2d2edbe-574d-44df-89b0-0176153c1f49-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 404, 'width': 600, 