value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-16T09:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Times Insider', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Bahr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bahr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ac7fd8f-dd47-527f-9b36-2f5dc0ea2b71', 'word_count': 676, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ac7fd8f-dd47-527f-9b36-2f5dc0ea2b71'}, {'abstract': 'In this world filled, sometimes, with more breakdowns than breaks, it feels sometimes an anachronism to hear anybody say they love somebody deeply.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/16/magazine/poem-waiting-on-you-to-die-so-i-can-be-myself.html', 'snippet': 'In this world filled, sometimes, with more breakdowns than breaks, it feels sometimes an anachronism to hear anybody say they love somebody deeply.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two lines keep bringing me back: “i’m waiting for a few folks/i love dearly to die so i can be myself.” In this world filled, sometimes, with more breakdowns than breaks, it feels sometimes an anachronism to hear anybody say they love somebody deeply. Then it hurts a bit to remember all that comes with love. Who among us ain’t been a bit afraid to be they whole entire self? Who doesn’t understand that desire? Selected by Reginald Dwayne Betts', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/19/magazine/19mag-poem/19mag-poem-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/19/magazine/19mag-poem/19mag-poem-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': 