ose is one of a series of vaccine decisions expected in the coming weeks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Food and Drug Administration is likely to authorize Pfizer-BioNTech booster shots this week for many Americans at high risk of falling seriously ill from the coronavirus, now that a key advisory committee has voted to recommend the measure.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/20/us/20virus-briefing-booster-lookahead1/20virus-briefing-booster-lookahead1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/20/us/20virus-briefing-booster-lookahead1/20virus-briefing-booster-lookahead1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/20/us/20virus-briefing-booster-lookahead1/20virus-briefing-booster-lookahead1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/20/us/20virus-briefing-booster-lookahead1/20virus-briefing-booster-lookahead1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/20/us/20virus-briefing-booster-lookahead1/20virus-briefing-booster-lookahead1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/09/20/us/20virus-briefing-booster-lookahead1/20virus-briefing-booster-lookahead1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/20/us/20virus-briefing-booster-lookahead1/20virus-briefing-booster-lookahead1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The F.D.A. is likely to make its long-awaited decision on Pfizer boosters this week.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Food and Drug Administration', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-20T08:52:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Thomas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/53563bcb-27e3-54e7-8882-c42e3629c44d', 'word_count': 910, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53563bcb-27e3-54e7-8882-c42e3629c44d'}, {'abstract': 'Legislation opposing critical race theory spread across the country this spring and summer. How will these laws affect you, your school and the nation?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/20/learning/teaching-critical-race-theory-schools.html', 'snippet': 'Legislation opposing critical race theory spread across the country this spring and summer. How will these laws affect you, your school and the nation?', 'lead_paragraph': 'To date, more than 20 states — including New Hampshire, Michigan, Texas, Florida, South Carolina and Arkansas — have introduced regulations that restrict teaching about race and racism. Some of these laws have taken aim at what has been labeled critical race theory — an academic legal framework for understanding racism in the United States developed during the 1980s — while others are crafted more broadly to address what they call “divisive concepts.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/06/16/arts/16HISTORY-SUB/merlin_189218118_f4b21565-b877-4e0c-8dbe-7e1ed04391d7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/06/16/arts/16HISTORY-SUB/merlin_189218118_f4b21565-b877-4e0c-8dbe-7e1ed04391d7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 368}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/06/16/arts/16HISTORY-SUB/merlin_189218118_f4b21565-b877-4e0c-8dbe-7e1ed04391d7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 627, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/06/16/arts/16HISTORY-SUB/merlin_189218118_f4b21565-b877-4e0c-8dbe-7e1ed04391d7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1254, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/06/16/arts/16HISTORY-SUB/16HISTORY-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/06/16/arts/16HISTORY-SUB/16HISTORY-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/06/16/arts/16HISTORY-SUB/16HISTORY-SUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Is Your Reaction to Efforts to Limit Teaching on Race in Schools?', 'kicker': 'student opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2021-09-20T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy Engle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Engle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5737dd18-7fc1-5fe6-9c5a-fb41989412b5', 'word_count': 1142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5737dd18-7fc1-5fe6-9c5a-fb41989412b5'}, {'abstract': 'C.E.O. Anne Wojcicki grapples with the ethical questions that come with building a business on customers’ personal information.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/20/opinion/23andme-dna.html', 'snippet': 'C.E.O. Anne Wojcicki grapples with the ethical questions that come with building a business on customers’ personal information.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anne Wojcicki is sitting on a treasure trove of genetic data. Wojcicki, a co-founder and the chief executive of the genetic testing company 23andMe, has led it through 14 years in which it has collected data from millions of customers through at-home DNA spit test kits. In 2018 the company announced a collaboration with GlaxoSmithKline to use this anonymized, aggregated data to develop pharmaceutical drugs —\xa0and attracted a $300 million investment from the pharmaceutical giant. And in June, when Wojcicki took the company public, it was valued at $3.5 billion. In some ways, it’s a standard Silicon Valley play: Lure customers in with the promise of democratizing information, then quickly moving to monetize that information. But what are the implications when the information at stake is your DNA?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/20/opinion/20sway-wojcicki-image/20sway-wojcicki-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/20/opinion/20sway-wojcicki-image/20sway-wojcicki-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/20/opinion/20sway-wojcicki-image/20sway-wojcicki-image-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/20/opinion/20sway-wojcicki-image/20sway-wojcicki-image-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/20/opinion/20sway-wojcicki-image/20sway-wojcicki-image-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/09/20/opinion/20sway-wojcicki-image/20sway-wojcicki-image-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/20/opinion/20sway-wojcicki-image/20sway-wojcicki-image-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Is 23andMe Doing With Your DNA?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': '23andMe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'GlaxoSmithKline PLC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wojcicki, Anne', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-20T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': "By ‘Sway'", 'person': [{'firstname': "Sway'", 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/6483c102-57b0-5351-86eb-54c1e4446182', 'word_count': 294, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6483c102-57b0-5351-86eb-54c1e4446182'}, {'abstract': 'We need a new definition of expertise that relies more on  track records and less on credentials and the narrow forms of knowledge  they reward. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/20/opinion/afghanistan-experts-expertise.html', 'snippet': 'We need a new definition of expertise that relies more on  track records and less on credentials and the narrow forms of knowledge  they reward. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'Much has been written about what the U.S. withdrawal from Afghanistan means for the future of that country and America’s global standing. But the failures of the war reveal a need for deeper introspection into what has gone wrong with American democracy and its institutions — including the story of failed expertise.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/18/reader-center/18hanania_1/merlin_194288775_febaef4c-88d7-4f9f-bcc9-eebd7a34b8d2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/18/reader-center/18hanania_1/merlin_194288775_febaef4c-88d7-4f9f-bcc9-eebd7a34b8d2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 't