, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Irreverent Novel Trains Its Gaze on Refugees and Their Rescuers', 'kicker': 'fiction', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Saviors With Selfies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Wrong End of the Telescope (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Alameddine, Rabih', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-21T09:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dina Nayeri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nayeri', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9360d81a-d69c-531d-9213-15e66a3958c8', 'word_count': 780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9360d81a-d69c-531d-9213-15e66a3958c8'}, {'abstract': 'The historian Leslie Reagan puts Texas’ latest abortion law in perspective.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/21/opinion/ezra-klein-podcast-leslie-reagan.html', 'snippet': 'The historian Leslie Reagan puts Texas’ latest abortion law in perspective.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few weeks ago, the Supreme Court let stand a Texas law creating a system of vigilante legal enforcement against anyone who participates in an abortion after the point of fetal cardiac activity. In effect, Texas’ law bans abortions after about six weeks, which is long before many women even know they’re pregnant. And soon the court will hear arguments on a Mississippi abortion ban that will give the justices the chance to overturn Roe v. Wade directly.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/21/opinion/21eks-reagan-image/21eks-reagan-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/21/opinion/21eks-reagan-image/21eks-reagan-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/21/opinion/21eks-reagan-image/21eks-reagan-image-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/21/opinion/21eks-reagan-image/21eks-reagan-image-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/21/opinion/21eks-reagan-image/21eks-reagan-image-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/09/21/opinion/21eks-reagan-image/21eks-reagan-image-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/21/opinion/21eks-reagan-image/21eks-reagan-image-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'We’re on the Precipice of a Post-Roe World', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'audio-neutral-informative', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Abortion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pregnancy and Childbirth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-21T09:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': "By ‘The Ezra Klein Show'", 'person': [], 'organization': "The Ezra Klein Show'"}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/11244020-d444-561d-acf3-14d40fe10da0', 'word_count': 318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11244020-d444-561d-acf3-14d40fe10da0'}, {'abstract': 'We need to acknowledge that it’s strange where right and left have ended up.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/21/opinion/covid-politics.html', 'snippet': 'We need to acknowledge that it’s strange where right and left have ended up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I want to put a text before you, from February 2020, the ideological landscape into which the coronavirus first arrived. It’s a review in The London Review of Books, a fine highbrow left-of-center publication, covering a book about plague and quarantine in 17th-century Italy. The book, by the University of London historian, John Henderson, details the attempts by the city of Florence — led by its public health board, the Sanità — to avoid the awful fate of other Italian cities: first by closing the city to commerce and then by imposing quarantines, lockdowns and what we now call social distancing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/21/opinion/21douthat1/21douthat1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/21/opinion/21douthat1/21douthat1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/21/opinion/21douthat1/21douthat1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/21/opinion/21douthat1/21douthat1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/21/opinion/21douthat1/21douthat1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/09/21/opinion/21douthat1/21douthat1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/21/opinion/21douthat1/21douthat1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Extremely Weird Politics of Covid', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coronavirus (2019-nCoV)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libertarianism (US Politics)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quarantines', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disease Rates', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Masks', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-21T09:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/16e121fb-d747-57ee-82c1-84bbc36d839d', 'word_count': 1774, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16e121fb-d747-57ee-82c1-84bbc36d839d'}, {'abstract': 'Three new historical novels remind us how what happened in days, and centuries, gone by connects to the world today.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/21/books/review/the-women-of-troy-pat-barker.html', 'snippet': 'Three new historical novels remind us how what happened in days, and centuries, gone by connects to the world today.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Do you read historical fiction to be immersed in an alternate reality? To imagine what past lives might have looked and felt like? To find connections to the present? Your answers to those questions will determine your reaction to THE WOMEN OF TROY (Doubleday, 304 pp., $28), the latest installment in Pat Barker’s feminist retelling of the “Iliad.” Narrated, like its predecessor, “The Silence of the Girls,” by Briseis, a captive Trojan queen, it’s set in the camp of the increasingly fractious Greeks, trapped after their victory by a ferocious wind that prevents them from returning home.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/books/review/15Historical_BECKER/15Historical_BECKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 326, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/15/books/review/15Historical_BECKER/15Historical_BECKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 326}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/books/review/15Historical_BECKER/15Historical_BECKER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 556, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/books/review/15Historical_BECKER/15Historical_BECKER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1111, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/books/review/15Historical_BECKER/15Historical_BECKER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/09/15/books/review/15Historical_BECKER/15Historical_BECKER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/15/books/review/15Historical_BECKER/15Historical_BECKER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Back to the Future, With Books Instead of a DeLorean', 'kicker': 'Historical Fiction', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Past Tense', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barker, Pat', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Manningtree Witches (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'China Room (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blakemore, AK', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sahota, Sunjeev', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-21T09:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alida Becker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alida', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Becker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f4af9ad-4ca3-54e3-bff3-53bc13b6303c', 'word_count': 982, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f4af9ad-4ca3-54e3-bff3-53bc13b6303c'}, {'abstract': '“The Body Scout,” “Light From Uncommon Stars” and “No Gods, No Monsters” tell stories of genetic dystopias, musical gifts and mythic creatures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/21/books/review/lincoln-michel-ryka-aoki-cadwell-turnbull.html', 'snippet': '“The Body Scout,” “Light From Uncommon Stars” and “No Gods, No Monsters” tell stories of genetic dystopias, musical gifts and mythic creatures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lincoln Michel’s debut novel, THE BODY SCOUT (Orbit, 356 pp., $27), blends noir, cyberpunk and sports into something at once timeless and original. In an unsettlingly near-future New York where extreme climate change has made cybernetic limbs and genetic modifications — called “upgrades” — necessary and commonplace, steep income inequality and horrifying medical debt are ever more prevalent. But the book’s not really about those things. First and foremost, it’s about baseball.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/10/10/books/review/21Otherworldly/21Otherworldly-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/10/10/books/review/21Otherworldly/21Otherworldly-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/10/10/books/review/21Otherworldly/21Otherworldly-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/10/10/books/review/21Otherworldly/21Otherworldly-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 