osen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab9749ca-a172-5508-9e28-f5fe2dccee01', 'word_count': 1228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab9749ca-a172-5508-9e28-f5fe2dccee01'}, {'abstract': 'In response to the colorful, zanier side of modern furniture, some American craftspeople are returning to elemental, straightforward and handmade pieces.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/21/t-magazine/contemporary-woodworking-furniture-design.html', 'snippet': 'In response to the colorful, zanier side of modern furniture, some American craftspeople are returning to elemental, straightforward and handmade pieces.', 'lead_paragraph': '“IF A CHAIR or a building is not functional, if it appears to be only art, it is ridiculous,” wrote the American artist and furniture maker Donald Judd in his 1993 essay “It’s Hard to Find a Good Lamp.” In the piece, he derides most commercially available furniture — too overstuffed, too bourgeois, too obsessed with the past — and explains why, in the early ’70s, he felt compelled to design his own objects, first to fill his homes in Marfa, Texas, and Manhattan, and later to sell. According to Judd, while art is conceived to exist purely on its own terms, a chair is to be sat upon; its form should “never violate” that function.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '72', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/21/t-magazine/21tmag-woodworkers-slide-R23T-copy/21tmag-woodworkers-slide-R23T-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/21/t-magazine/21tmag-woodworkers-slide-R23T-copy/21tmag-woodworkers-slide-R23T-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 