izet', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ceramics and Pottery', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-23T09:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gautier Billotte', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gautier', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Billotte', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/3a4652aa-16d4-584f-8e88-37dbec75a7e6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/3a4652aa-16d4-584f-8e88-37dbec75a7e6'}, {'abstract': 'The French artist Agnès Debizet populates every nook of her rustic estate outside of Paris with her fantastical forms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/23/t-magazine/agnes-debizet-home-studio.html', 'snippet': 'The French artist Agnès Debizet populates every nook of her rustic estate outside of Paris with her fantastical forms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AS YOU PASS through the cast-iron gates that lead to the artist Agnès Debizet’s country studio on an old farm in the French village of Saint-Maurice-aux-Riches-Hommes, it can feel that you’re entering another world altogether. Ninety minutes northwest is Paris, where Debizet, 63, owns an apartment in the Marais, but here, across a group of 19th-century farm buildings that surround a grassy courtyard, are more than 150 sculptures from her 40-year-long career. Inside the courtyard sits “Évolution” (2007-15), a monumental installation of approximately 40 stoneware sculptures that gradually increase in size, from one that resembles an ostrich egg to eight-foot towers that recall morel mushrooms, all a sun-bleached white with a porous, coral-like texture. Currently, the work is arranged in a circle surrounding a linden tree, but Debizet is constantly changing its configuration (a snaking line, a chaotic pile), something she does with the majority of her creations.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '86', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/23/t-magazine/23tmag-debizet-slide-DG0E-copy/23tmag-debizet-slide-DG0E-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/23/t-magazine/23tmag-debizet-slide-DG0E-copy/23tmag-debizet-slide-DG0E-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/23/t-magazine/23tmag-debizet-slide-DG0E-copy/23tmag-debizet-slide-DG0E-copy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/23/t-magazine/23tmag-debizet-slide-DG0E-copy/23tmag-debizet-slide-DG0E-copy-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1098, 'width': 1648, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/23/t-magazine/23tmag-debizet-slide-DG0E-copy/23tmag-debizet-slide-DG0E-copy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/09/23/t-magazine/23tmag-debizet-slide-DG0E-copy/23tmag-debizet-slide-DG0E-copy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/23/t-magazine/23tmag-debizet-slide-DG0E-copy/23tmag-debizet-slide-DG0E-copy-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On a 19