rds': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-23T12:48:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jill Cowan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/d99c2101-8b4e-5a1d-8e91-0343f1adcfec', 'word_count': 1407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d99c2101-8b4e-5a1d-8e91-0343f1adcfec'}, {'abstract': 'A reader asks whether a defamatory post on social media is cause for disinviting a dinner guest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/23/style/social-media-defamation.html', 'snippet': 'A reader asks whether a defamatory post on social media is cause for disinviting a dinner guest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As soon as everyone was vaccinated, we invited my family to our home for a holiday meal outdoors. We also included my brother-in-law, who is my wife’s only living relative and has nowhere else to go. My own brother expressed surprise at this: Unbeknown to us, my brother-in-law had unfriended us on Facebook and posted a rant accusing us of elder abuse that led to his mother’s death. (We cared for her in our home until she died, and I’m proud of the years we took care of her.) Knowing this, I would like to disinvite him. But my wife says that she’s ignored similarly awful statements from him in the past, and there’s no point in arguing with him: He never admits he’s wrong. What should we do?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/06/20/fashion/socialqs-2021-artwork/socialqs-2021-artwork-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images