': None, 'lastname': 'Paton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e614a5f-eaf1-5b7c-a749-4b400ae3cab6', 'word_count': 879, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e614a5f-eaf1-5b7c-a749-4b400ae3cab6'}, {'abstract': 'The film director set himself a steep challenge in his debut stage work. At least for now, he hasn’t quite met it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/24/theater/andy-gus-van-sant-warhol-review.html', 'snippet': 'The film director set himself a steep challenge in his debut stage work. At least for now, he hasn’t quite met it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LISBON — Gus Van Sant is no stranger to experimental biopics: “Last Days,” his lyrical, nearly dialogue-free meditation on the end of Kurt Cobain’s life, shunned every convention of the genre. Yet “Andy,” his Andy Warhol-inspired stage debut, which had its world premiere at the Teatro Nacional D. Maria II in Lisbon this week, may be Van Sant’s oddest tribute to date.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/24/theater/24andy-review01/merlin_195034248_29ea6a48-3fe8-4e5d-909a-83c61c132a52-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/24/theater/24andy-review01/merlin_195034248_29ea6a48-3fe8-4e5d-909a-83c61c132a52-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/24/theater/24andy-review01/merlin_195034248_29ea6a48-3fe8-4e5d-909a-83c61c132a52-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/24/theater/24andy-review01/merlin_195034248_29ea6a48-3fe8-4e5d-909a-83c61c132a52-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/24/theater/24andy-review01/24andy-review01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/09/24/theater/24andy-review01/24andy-review01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/24/theater/24andy-review01/24andy-review01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Andy,’ Gus Van Sant’s Warhol Musical, Is a Surprise but Not a Miracle', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'van Sant, Gus', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warhol, Andy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Andy (Play)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-24T13:39:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Cappelle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cappelle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/777e4e03-c22c-5845-ac6f-0278614dd140', 'word_count': 811, 'uri': 'nyt://article/777e4e03-c22c-5845-ac6f-0278614dd140'}, {'abstract': 'Following a period of editing and rediscovery, austerity and abundance — and a wide range of objects — live side by side in this Bedford-Stuyvesant apartment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/24/t-magazine/brooklyn-apartment-design-speaklow.html', 'snippet': 'Following a period of editing and rediscovery, austerity and abundance — and a wide range of objects — live side by side in this Bedford-Stuyvesant apartment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AS A CHILD in Virginia, Michael Brown collected birds’ nests, stamps and mounted beetles and butterflies. By the age of 10, he had graduated to Goofus glass vases — cold-painted trinkets produced in the early 20th century that were often given as fairground prizes — and old-fashioned spectacle frames he’d find at local flea markets. His first job, at 18, was dressing windows at the Richmond department store Thalhimers, and on the weekends he began collecting antique furniture and curiosities, a habit that continued into adulthood, as he worked as an interior stylist and retail art director, picking up treasures wherever he went: a 1920s lacquered gold Japanese screen from a stint living in Portland, Ore.; sun-bleached sea turtle shells from a vacation in Maine. By the time the 59-year-old found his current home in 2013, a 1,000-square-foot, one-bedroom rental on the top floor of a brownstone in the Bedford-Stuyvesant neighborhood of Brooklyn, he needed a 20-foot truck to transport the goods he’d accumulated. “I wish I could be one of those people who could buy a thing, live with it, throw it out and move on,” he says. “But I can’t.”', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/24/t-magazine/24tmag-speaklow-slide-VH0S-copy/24tmag-speaklow-slide-VH0S-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/24/t-magazine/24tmag-speaklow-slide-VH0S-copy/24tmag-speaklow-slide-VH0S-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/24/t-magazine/24tmag-speaklow-slide-VH0S-copy/24tmag-speaklow-slide-VH0S-copy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/24/t-magazine/24tmag-speaklow-slide-VH0S-copy/24tmag-speaklow-slide-VH0S-copy-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1251, 'width': 1877, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/24/t-magazine/24tmag-speaklow-slide-VH0S-copy/24tmag-speaklow-slide-VH0S-copy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2021/09/24/t-magazine/24tmag-speaklow-slide-VH0S-copy/24tmag-speaklow-slide-VH0S-copy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/24/t-magazine/24tmag-speaklow-slide-VH0S-copy/24tmag-speaklow-slide-VH0S-copy-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Brooklyn, a Couple With Opposing Tastes Strike a Balance', 'kicker': 'At Home', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More Or Less', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brownstones', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bedford-Stuyvesant (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antiques', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', '