lue': 'Coronavirus Risks and Safety Concerns', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-24T21:50:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tressie McMillan Cottom', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tressie', 'middlename': 'Mcmillan', 'lastname': 'Cottom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/a749c308-a62f-5e8d-89ef-d84c71fa9ea9', 'word_count': 1366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a749c308-a62f-5e8d-89ef-d84c71fa9ea9'}, {'abstract': 'Pedro Martinez is leaving San Antonio to become Chicago’s schools chief. He weighs in on his battles with Gov. Greg Abbott and why a student vaccine mandate should be “on the table.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/24/us/pedro-martinez-greg-abbott-texas.html', 'snippet': 'Pedro Martinez is leaving San Antonio to become Chicago’s schools chief. He weighs in on his battles with Gov. Greg Abbott and why a student vaccine mandate should be “on the table.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'For months, Pedro Martinez has been locked in a political battle over the safety of schools in Texas. As the superintendent in San Antonio, he defied Gov. Greg Abbott by issuing a mask mandate in schools. He was the only superintendent of a major Texas school district to require school employees to be vaccinated, prompting a lawsuit from the Texas attorney general.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/24/us/24schools-martinez-1/merlin_194794839_e62898c9-2edc-4fdb-9d44-c7fd08197f40-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/24/us/24schools-martinez-1/merlin_194794839_e62898c9-2edc-4fdb-9d44-c7fd08197f40-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlar