: 'Leonhardt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/44cc2d2a-410d-55e4-a4f5-1adcc7b2e9c3', 'word_count': 1638, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44cc2d2a-410d-55e4-a4f5-1adcc7b2e9c3'}, {'abstract': 'After a loving restoration, the home of Rafael Urzúa Arias, one of Mexico’s leading 20th-century architects, is once again a livable residence, and honors his spirit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/27/t-magazine/rafael-urzua-arias-home-architecture.html', 'snippet': 'After a loving restoration, the home of Rafael Urzúa Arias, one of Mexico’s leading 20th-century architects, is once again a livable residence, and honors his spirit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN THE MID-1940s, while serving as the director of public works in Guadalajara, Mexico, the architect Rafael Urzúa Arias slated the first two houses he had ever designed for demolition in order to widen what would become one of the city’s main roads. When the homes were finally torn down in 1952, colleagues asked why he hadn’t chosen another street, a decision that might have better preserved his legacy. Urzúa, then 47, responded with a proverb: “El buen juez por su casa empieza” — “The good judge begins with his own home.”', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '123', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/27/t-magazine/27tmag-urzua-slide-W8WG/27tmag-urzua-slide-W8WG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/27/t-magazine/27tmag-urzua-slide-W8WG/27tmag-urzua-slide-W8WG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/27/t-magazine/27tmag-urzua-slide-W8WG/27tmag-urzua-slide-W