er', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Topoleanu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bff0486-3638-5b77-a6c8-ebc01396ebfd', 'word_count': 1345, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bff0486-3638-5b77-a6c8-ebc01396ebfd'}, {'abstract': 'Too many people remain stubbornly stuck to the steady diet of lies they’ve been fed for months.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/27/opinion/when-medical-ethics-collide-with-basic-fairness.html', 'snippet': 'Too many people remain stubbornly stuck to the steady diet of lies they’ve been fed for months.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NASHVILLE — It’s hard for me to describe the utter rage that filled me when I opened my local newspaper last Tuesday and saw The Tennessean’s lead article: “Vaccinated Lose Access to Treatment,” the headline read. What new through-the-looking-glass madness was afoot in this Covid-beleaguered leadership vacuum?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/27/opinion/27renkl1/27renkl1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/27/opinion/27renkl1/27renkl1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/27/opinion/27renkl1/27renkl1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/27/opinion/27renkl1/27renkl1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'