leen C', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-28T09:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sharon Otterman and Joseph Goldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sharon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Otterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71499c26-9195-5130-bfe6-e38d3ad79ac3', 'word_count': 1628, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71499c26-9195-5130-bfe6-e38d3ad79ac3'}, {'abstract': 'We can’t make social media safe for teenagers. So we should keep them off.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/28/opinion/instagram-social-media-children.html', 'snippet': 'We can’t make social media safe for teenagers. So we should keep them off.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The rise of big tech and social media presents a series of difficult, perhaps intractable problems for Western societies. Our internet behemoths are effectively immense media companies pretending to be neutral platforms, feasting on the revenue that once sustained the old media ecosystem while disclaiming normal forms of editorial responsibility. Their key products are agents of decentralized suspicion, generating information overload and feeding both populist paranoia and centrist hysteria. Meanwhile, their leaders run transnational pseudo-governments, exerting traditional political powers — cultural censorship, political banishment, the structuring of vast marketplaces — without clear lines of political accountability.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/28/opinion/28douthat1/merlin_185493537_4836