 None, 'lastname': 'Yoon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Aanya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wipulasena', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79649259-8927-5774-9dd7-2f49fcb11455', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79649259-8927-5774-9dd7-2f49fcb11455'}, {'abstract': 'Some of the most innovative set designers and directors are placing actors within transparent boxes, posing novel aesthetic questions in the process.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/28/t-magazine/theater-sets-glass-boxes.html', 'snippet': 'Some of the most innovative set designers and directors are placing actors within transparent boxes, posing novel aesthetic questions in the process.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN A WORLD filtered through screens, a condition made even more acute during pandemic lockdown, the theater’s most anachronistic thrill would seem to be watching lives unfold before us. The actors may not literally be within our grasp, but the lack of a barrier between them and us, the illusion that we are, for once, actually in the room — the sound of the human voice in anguish or joy, a carafe of water crashing to the floor — has never seemed more stirring and essential.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '100', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/28/t-magazine/28tmag-glassbox-slide-97JF-copy/28tmag-glassbox-slide-97JF-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/09/28/t-magazine/28tmag-glassbox-slide-97JF-copy/28tmag-glassbox-slide-97JF-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'typ