ation': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3def525-5962-56c2-8daa-5f6943920c49', 'word_count': 102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3def525-5962-56c2-8daa-5f6943920c49'}, {'abstract': 'A bounty of clarity in the poetry of Rita Dove.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/30/magazine/poem-postlude.html', 'snippet': 'A bounty of clarity in the poetry of Rita Dove.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a poem of mine, I once borrowed the lines “If you can’t be free, be a mystery” from Rita Dove’s “Canary.” Using the lines, I possessed, for a moment, a rare kind of clarity. You gotta know who you are and who people imagine you to be, to really be free in this world. This new collection, “Playlist for the Apocalypse,” is a bounty of such moments. Like this one, in “Postlude,” where Dove doesn’t stop at “you prefer me invisible,” but reminds you (America, maybe) that “this is my business.” Selected by Reginald Dwayne Betts', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/10/03/magazine/03mag-poem/03mag-poem-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/10/03/magazine/03mag-poem/03mag-poem-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/10/03/magazine/03mag-poem/03mag-poem-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/10/03/magazine/03mag-poem/03mag-poem-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'ty