ews_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd0e432f-a569-5556-8abf-b7678f83c32e', 'word_count': 1020, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd0e432f-a569-5556-8abf-b7678f83c32e'}, {'abstract': 'After her offers of compensation were initially denied, a reader was surprised to hear her relative ask for back pay.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/30/style/child-care-family-pay.html', 'snippet': 'After her offers of compensation were initially denied, a reader was surprised to hear her relative ask for back pay.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In January, my husband’s sister drove cross-country to help care for our two young sons while my husband was deployed in the armed services. When I proposed the idea, I asked her to think about the pay she would like and the services she would provide. I told her we would cover her room and board. She never responded. After she arrived, I asked her several more times about pay and duties. She said she preferred an informal arrangement. Since then, she has helped out when she liked. Now, she is ready to drive home again and has asked for back pay. We are so grateful she came, but if I’d known I was paying her, I would have formalized her duties instead of accepting piecemeal help. What should I do?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/06/20/fashion/socialqs-2021-artwork/socialqs-2021-artwork-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2021/06/20/fashion/socialqs-2021-artwork/socialqs-2021-artwork-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 