jor': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Food and Commercial Workers Union', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tyson Foods Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2021-09-30T13:03:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lauren Hirsch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lauren', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hirsch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6289973-6603-5456-a6c5-1a66c6d161ca', 'word_count': 460, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6289973-6603-5456-a6c5-1a66c6d161ca'}, {'abstract': 'Fashion week may be back in-person, but not every designer wants to give up the digital approach. Marine Serre, Thebe Magugu, Burberry and Dries Van Noten explore the connection.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2021/09/30/style/netflix-shows.html', 'snippet': 'Fashion week may be back in-person, but not every designer wants to give up the digital approach. Marine Serre, Thebe Magugu, Burberry and Dries Van Noten explore the connection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — The last year and a half of being stuck to the small screen for work and pleasure, desperate for any new piece of escapism be it blockbuster or art house or glossy series, has to have changed forever our relationship to the moving picture, raising the stakes and the expectations. And if, when fashion first went online, the idea of transforming a show into a video seemed like a potential savior for the industry, it also exposed some of the limits in the fashion imagination.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2021/09/30/fashion/30SERRE-marine-serre-1/merlin_195541881_5464148a-8cf1-44d8-a0da-d29da7